        "content": "{\n  \"rule\": \"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged and a new passage of about 250 words in the text field.\",\n  \"text\": \"When the reservoir fell that summer, the old road appeared first, running pale and crooked into the mud.\\n\\nElsie parked beside the warning sign. Her grandson carried the folding chair she had told him she wouldn’t need.\\n\\n“Which house was yours?” he asked.\\n\\n“There aren’t houses now.”\\n\\n“I meant where.”\\n\\nShe pointed beyond a line of broken posts.\\n\\nThey walked slowly. Small shells cracked under his trainers. Elsie kept to the road, though the fields on either side looked firm enough.\\n\\nAt the bridge, she stopped.\\n\\nHe unfolded the chair.\\n\\n“I’m not tired.”\\n\\n“I am,” he said, and sat down.\\n\\nThe stream beneath the bridge was narrow enough to step across. A plastic bottle turned in a pool beside the stonework.\\n\\n“Your grandad dropped his bicycle here,” she said. “Showing off.”\\n\\n“Did you know him then?”\\n\\n“Not officially.”\\n\\nHe waited, but she was looking towards the posts again.\\n\\nAfter a while she took a key from her coat pocket. It was tied with string to a brown cardboard label, the writing almost gone.\\n\\n“Mum says you’ve still got that.”\\n\\n“Apparently.”\\n\\n“Does she know you brought it?”\\n\\nElsie rubbed a little rust from the teeth with her thumb.\\n\\nBelow them, the bottle knocked against the bridge. Her grandson climbed down and picked it up, letting the water drain before putting it in his bag.\\n\\nWhen he returned, she was sitting in the chair.\\n\\n“Found anything else?” she asked.\\n\\n“Just that.”\\n\\nShe held out her hand for help getting up. He steadied the chair while she folded it, then took it from her.\\n\\nThe key went back into her pocket.\"\n}",
